D.append(dict(description='Historian of American culture whose studies ranged from Salem witchcraft to nuclear fears and apocalyptic belief. Ben Alpers reads Salem Possessed as a productive but limited application of social history to wealth and landholding,and By the Bomb’s Early Light as recovering both hopes and fears about atomic power. The subjects connect interpretation to social and moral conflict without defining a single Boyer school.',scope='Named reader Ben Alpers’s2012 substantive discussion inspected; his reservations about Salem’s explanation retained. Alpers explicitly had not read Urban Masses,so no substantive claim about that book is inferred from his note.',sources=[S('Ben Alpers,Paul S.Boyer1935–2012,Society for US Intellectual History2 April2012','https://s-usih.org/2012/04/paul-s-boyer-1935-2012/','Named reader’s paragraphs on Salem,atomic hopes/fears and premillennialism read; irrelevant injected advertising ignored. Alpers’s stated non-reading of Urban Masses retained.'),S('Wisconsin History: Paul Boyer faculty memorial resolution','https://history.wisc.edu/people-main/in-memoriam/','Boyer section dates Salem Possessed1974 with Stephen Nissenbaum,By Bomb1985,When Time1992; explicit17 March2012 death.')],works=[W(1974,'Salem Possessed (with Stephen Nissenbaum)',1),W(1985,'By the Bomb’s Early Light',1),W(1992,'When Time Shall Be No More',1)],edges=[E('social','Salem Possessed applies landholding and wealth analysis to witchcraft accusations and local conflict.','Ben Alpers’s2012 substantial reading; he finds the explanatory model ingenious but not wholly convincing. Stephen Nissenbaum’s coauthorship retained.',0),E('culture','Boyer investigates atomic hope,fear and apocalyptic belief as historically consequential forms of American culture.','Alpers’s discussion of By the Bomb’s Early Light and When Time Shall Be No More; no dismissal of belief as mere pathology or complete cultural consensus.',0)],life=L('deceased',1,'Boyer memorial opening','Explicit17 March2012.',2012,'2012-03-17')))
D.append(dict(description='Historian of international relations who brought cultural exchange and non-state organizations into accounts of world order. In his interview with Peter Frost,Iriye distinguishes state power from cultural initiatives while recognizing their interaction and the wartime subordination of culture to power. His internationalization of history argues for cross-national inquiry rather than a history bounded by one country’s perspective.',sources=[S('Akira Iriye,The Internationalization of History,AHA address28 December1988,AHR94(1989),pp1–10','https://www.historians.org/presidential-address/akira-iriye/','Own opening and methodological call for international historical inquiry inspected; delivery1988 distinct from1989 publication.'),S('Peter Frost interviews Akira Iriye,Education About Asia3/2(1998)','https://www.educationaboutasia.org/article/id/162/print/','Own power/culture/economics answers and concluding cultural-internationalism statement read; bibliography dates After Imperialism1965,Cultural Internationalism1997. Tentativeness and continuing state power explicit.'),S('Harvard faculty memorial minute: Akira Iriye,7 May2026','https://news.harvard.edu/gazette/story/2026/05/akira-iriye-91/','Explicit death27 January2026; Global Community2002 publication and international-organizations research identified.')],works=[W(1965,'After Imperialism',1),W(1997,'Cultural Internationalism and World Order',1),W(2002,'Global Community',2)],edges=[E('global','Iriye advocates cross-national historical inquiry that exceeds a single nation’s archives and perspective.','Own1988/1989 address opening; scholarly internationalization is a method and aspiration,not proof of a unified worldwide narrative.',0),E('culture','Iriye’s cultural internationalism examines non-state exchanges and organizations alongside state power.','Own1998 Frost interview distinguishes society-based initiatives from state power and notes culture can also serve war; no assumption that exchange necessarily produces peace.',1)],life=L('deceased',2,'Life-date heading','Explicit27 January2026.',2026,'2026-01-27')))
D.append(dict(description='Historian of slavery and abolition whose comparative intellectual history treated bondage as central to Western and Atlantic modernity. In his2008 retrospective,Davis asks how moral perception and organized opinion changed while slavery remained economically powerful. His interpretation gives ideas a major role in abolition; that emphasis,and his assessment of enslaved revolt,remain arguments rather than a settled hierarchy of causes.',scope='Own2008 lecture printed in Proceedings118/2(2008),copyright2009,selected pp247–253 and265–266 inspected. Publication/lecture/copyright dates distinguished; full trilogy and middle lecture sections not read.',sources=[S('David Brion Davis,Re-Examining the Problem of Slavery in Western Culture,Proceedings American Antiquarian Society118/2(2008),pp247–266','https://americanantiquarian.org/proceedings/45147497.pdf','Own opening/research origins pp247–253 and conclusion265–266 read. Moral-perception thesis explicitly Davis’s position; he says revolts can be exaggerated,not adopted here as a consensus.'),S('Yale News: David Brion Davis obituary15 April2019','https://news.yale.edu/2019/04/15/david-brion-davis-pulitzer-prize-winner-and-renowned-historian-slavery','Explicit death14 April2019; original Western Culture1966,Age Revolution1975,Inhuman Bondage2006 dates.')],works=[W(1966,'The Problem of Slavery in Western Culture',1),W(1975,'The Problem of Slavery in the Age of Revolution',1),W(2006,'Inhuman Bondage',1)],edges=[E('intellectualhistory','Davis treats abolition as a problem of changing moral perception and public argument rather than the automatic effect of economic decline.','Own2008 conclusion265–266; intellectual causation is his defended interpretation,not the removal of material interests or resistance.',0),E('atlantic','Davis’s comparison places American slavery and abolition in wider hemispheric and European histories.','Own2008 pp252–253 and265–266; Western cultural focus and contested weighting of revolt remain limits,not universal coverage.',0)],life=L('deceased',1,'Opening obituary','Explicit14 April2019.',2019,'2019-04-14')))
D.append(dict(description='Historian of American labour,political economy and national belonging. Gerstle’s concept of a political order explains how a dominant movement can require opponents to accept its basic terms. His account of neoliberalism links bipartisan political settlement to capitalism’s globalization and later crises,while leaving the form of a successor order open. Earlier work connects labour and nationalism rather than assuming patriotic language is inherently conservative.',sources=[S('Gary Gerstle interviewed by Rob Johnson,INET9 June2022,transcript','https://www.ineteconomics.org/perspectives/podcasts/the-rise-and-fall-of-the-neoliberal-order','Own opening answers on political orders,Reagan/Clinton,capitalism and transnational comparison read. Predictions remain uncertain and interview date distinct from events.'),S('Gary Gerstle author biography','https://www.garygerstle.com/about/','Original Working-Class Americanism1989 and coedited New Deal Order1989 bibliography; contemporary author profile identifies research projects.',True),S('British National Bibliography: American Crucible','https://obnb.uk/p11976362-american-crucible-race-and-nation-in-the-twentieth-century','Princeton original2001 publication distinguished from updated2017 edition.',True),S('UTS,After neoliberalism—what comes next?,15 October2026','https://www.uts.edu.au/news/2026/10/uts-democracy-forum','University report of Gerstle’s recent Democracy Forum speech; positive current life evidence.')],works=[W(1989,'Working-Class Americanism',1),W(2001,'American Crucible',2),W(2022,'The Rise and Fall of the Neoliberal Order',0,'Contemporaneous2022 author interview identifies new book; date corroborated by current author profile.')],edges=[E('political','Gerstle’s political-order concept explains durable settlements whose assumptions constrain governing parties and their opponents.','Own2022 opening definition and New Deal/neoliberal comparison; historical model rather than a fixed law or inevitable sequence.',0),E('capitalismdebates','Gerstle links neoliberal ascendancy and breakdown to political choices,globalization and crises of market legitimacy.','Own2022 transcript on Reagan,Clinton and global capitalism; future successor remains indeterminate.',0)],life=L('living',3,'15 October2026 forum report','Positive university report of recent speech.')))
D.append(dict(description='International historian whose Cold War research places superpower conflict within global ideological competition and postcolonial transformations. Westad’s account follows interventions beyond Europe and examines how capitalism and socialism shaped choices. His2017 explanation emphasizes newly accessible sources and synthesis across regional scholarship; global scope does not make local actors passive or every present rivalry a repeated Cold War.',sources=[S('Odd Arne Westad on The Cold War:A World History,Harvard Kennedy School Behind the Book2017,transcript','https://www.hks.harvard.edu/behind-the-book/odd-arne-westads-cold-war-world-history','Own transcript on research access,global synthesis and late-nineteenth-century ideological divides read; he distinguishes later conflicts from the Cold War.'),S('Odd Arne Westad,The Global Cold War chapter4 opening,Cambridge2005','https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/global-cold-war/creating-the-third-world-the-united-states-confronts-revolution/C1BA628C018DA27531BDB25BF4EDADF6','Author chapter opening on US anti-communism,intervention capability and postwar Third World read in public extract; later chapter not read.'),S('Yale Jackson: Odd Arne Westad biography and bibliography','https://jackson.yale.edu/directory/odd-arne-westad','Current teaching/directorships and2026book; dates Decisive Encounters2003,Global Cold War2005,Cold War2017.',True),S('Giorgos Skafidas interviews Odd Arne Westad,Kathimerini9 May2026','https://www.ekathimerini.com/in-depth/interviews-in-depth/1303104/','Positive dated direct interview; contemporary forecasts not used as established historical outcomes.')],works=[W(2003,'Decisive Encounters',2),W(2005,'The Global Cold War',2),W(2017,'The Cold War:A World History',2)],edges=[E('global','Westad frames Cold War history through global ideological divides and the synthesis of research across regions.','Own2017 HKS transcript; temporal reach to nineteenth-century ideologies is distinguished from a simple extension of every Cold War event.',0),E('post','Westad investigates how postwar superpower interventions intersected with anticolonial change beyond Europe.','Own2005 chapter4 public opening specifies US perceptions,anti-communism and intervention capacity; this is a contribution to colonial/postcolonial history,not automatic membership in a theoretical school.',1)],life=L('living',3,'9 May2026 interview','Positive dated direct interview,identity matched through Yale and books.')))
