D.append(dict(description='Historian of early America who made the relation between slavery and freedom a central interpretive problem. In his1972 address,Morgan rejects treating enslavement as an exception to the national story or freedom as mere disguise. His Virginia-centered argument asks how racial domination and the independence claimed by white colonists developed together. Its particular mechanism remains open to debate rather than explaining every colonial society.',scope='Own1972 address selected opening pp5–8 inspected; complete article and monographs not read. Bibliography preserves Helen M.Morgan’s coauthorship of The Stamp Act Crisis.',sources=[S('Edmund S.Morgan,Slavery and Freedom:The American Paradox,JAH59/1(June1972),pp5–29','https://academic.oup.com/jah/article-abstract/59/1/5/840298','Own pp5–8 read in public scan https://whataboutthekennedys.com/wp-content/uploads/2012/04/slavery-and-freedom-the-american-paradox.pdf; delivery6 April1972 distinct from June publication.'),S('UNC Press:The Stamp Act Crisis','https://uncpress.org/9780807845134/the-stamp-act-crisis/','Original1953 explicitly distinguished from new-preface1995edition; Edmund S.and Helen M.Morgan both authors.',True),S('WorldCat:Inventing the People,OCLC16581158','https://search.worldcat.org/title/Inventing-the-people-%3A-the-rise-of-popular-sovereignty-in-England-and-America/oclc/16581158','Norton copyright1988 matched Edmund S.Morgan author.',True),S('John Murrin,Edmund Sears Morgan1916–2013,AHA December2013','https://www.historians.org/perspectives-article/edmund-sears-morgan-1916-2013-december-2013/','Institutional scholarly obituary dates American Slavery American Freedom1975 and death8 July2013; named attribution checked.')],works=[W(1953,'The Stamp Act Crisis (with Helen M.Morgan)',1),W(1975,'American Slavery,American Freedom',3),W(1988,'Inventing the People',2)],edges=[E('political','Morgan’s American paradox connects revolutionary commitments to independence with the simultaneous development of slaveholding society.','Own1972 pp5–8 rejects both simple hypocrisy and a story that treats slavery as an exception; Virginia-centered mechanism is bounded.',0),E('racial_formation','Morgan places racial domination within the formation of colonial freedom and social independence.','Own1972 pp5–8 explicitly recognizes racism while asking about the material/social conditions of independence; not a universal account of every colony.',0)],life=L('deceased',3,'Opening obituary','Explicit8 July2013.',2013,'2013-07-08')))
D.append(dict(description='Historian of collective mentalities and the French Revolution who connected long changes in death and religion to moments of political rupture. In La Mentalité révolutionnaire,Vovelle asks whether slow transformations can accelerate in revolutionary crisis. His analysis treats hope and fear,violence and fraternity as interrelated,and explicitly rejects both unconditional devotion to the Revolution and blanket denunciation.',scope='Own La Mentalité révolutionnaire selected pp233–235 read in2012 reprint of1991article. Original/reprint dates kept separate; whole book and remaining article not read.',sources=[S('Michel Vovelle,La Mentalité révolutionnaire,Historyka special issue2012,pp233–245,reprinting1991article','https://www.journals.pan.pl/Content/109576/PDF/13_Vovelle.pdf?handler=pdf','Own opening pp233–235 on long/short duration,dechristianization,and dialectic of hope/fear read. Reprint header identifies original1991 HistorykaXXI pp3–14.'),S('BnF:Idéologies et mentalités,FRBNF34688136','https://catalogue.bnf.fr/ark%3A/12148/cb34688136t','Paris F.Maspero1982; collection of earlier1978–82texts,not all first written1982.',True),S('Mucem:Piété baroque et déchristianisation en Provence','https://mucem.org/notice/b/oai%3APMB%3A57224/','Plon1973book distinguished from1971doctoral/three-volume manifestation.',True),S('IHMC:Hommage à Michel Vovelle','https://ihmc.ens.psl.eu/hommage-michel-vovelle.html','Explicit deathSaturday6 October2018; identity historian of Revolution and Bicentenary.'),S('Sorbonne Library:Hommage à Michel Vovelle','https://www.bis-sorbonne.fr/mediabis-hub/hommage-michel-vovelle-bibliographie','Conflicting death5 October2018 recorded; same historian. Year2018 adopted conservatively.')],works=[W(1973,'Piété baroque et déchristianisation en Provence',2,'1973Plon edition distinguished from1971thesis.'),W(1982,'Idéologies et mentalités',1,'1982collection; component essays1978–82 perBnF.'),W(1991,'La Mentalité révolutionnaire (article)',0,'Original1991article date from2012reprint header; reprint is not original.')],edges=[E('annales','Vovelle relates long-duration collective mentalities to the rapid transformations of revolutionary crisis.','Own1991/2012 opening explicitly engages Mandrou and Braudel; not a claim that all Annales historians shared his revolutionary interpretation.',0),E('history_of_emotions','Vovelle analyzes revolutionary hope and fear as related collective forces rather than isolated psychological traits.','Own pp234–235 discussion of Lefebvre’s paired concepts; a specific earlier resource for emotion history,not sole foundation or later professional self-identification.',0)],life=dict(status='deceased',death_year=2018,death_date=None,death_precision='year',sources=[[3,'Opening tribute','6 October2018'],[4,'Opening library tribute','5 October2018']],limits='Institutional witnesses disagree5/6October2018; retain year only. No work or influence interval extended to death.')))
D.append(dict(description='Historian of the American South whose writing links slavery,women’s wartime choices and the cultural consequences of mass death. Faust’s interview with Jim Leach explains how slaveholders’ records can reveal enslaved family ties and how Confederate women’s exhaustion changed their relation to war. Her biographical work uses an individual life to investigate social structures while preserving the distinction between understanding and endorsement.',sources=[S('Jim Leach interviews Drew Gilpin Faust,NEH Jefferson Lecture2011 page','https://www.neh.gov/about/awards/jefferson-lecture/drew-gilpin-faust-biography','Own interview sections on enslaved family ties,Hammond biography,women’s exhaustion and culture/meaning read. David W.Blight’s separately headed appreciation not attributed to Faust.'),S('Drew Gilpin Faust CV,as of20 February2026','https://bpb-us-e1.wpmucdn.com/websites.harvard.edu/dist/2/109/files/2026/03/CURRENT-DGF-CV-revised-February-2026.pdf','Books pp3–4:A Sacred Circle1977,Mothers1996,Republic2008; later paperbacks distinct. Current2026authorCV supports life; apparent Creation Confederate Nationalism1982typo not adopted.',True),S('Harvard History:Drew Faust current faculty biography','https://history.fas.harvard.edu/people/drew-faust','Current research-professor role,contact and2023memoir identify historian/former president; positive institutional life evidence.')],works=[W(1977,'A Sacred Circle',1),W(1996,'Mothers of Invention',1),W(2008,'This Republic of Suffering',1)],edges=[E('women','Faust treats slaveholding women’s changing wartime interests as consequential for Confederate endurance and collapse.','Own2011Leach answer on exhaustion and family/property preservation; scoped to slaveholding women rather than all Southern women.',0),E('biography','Faust’s Hammond biography uses an individual’s extensive records to investigate slavery and social power in the Old South.','Own2011answers explain biography as a window onto society and reconstruct enslaved kinship from records; understanding the subject does not endorse his values.',0)],life=L('living',1,'CV header and20 February2026as-of date','Positive current2026authorCV corroborated by Harvard profile.')))
